          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "How to Use a Portable Generator",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/how-to-use-a-portable-generator/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do you use a portable generator step by step?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Place it outdoors, around twenty feet from the house with the exhaust pointing away. Check oil and fuel. Start it with nothing connected and let it warm for two to three minutes. Connect loads one at a time, largest starting draw first. To stop, disconnect loads, let it run a minute at no load, then switch off."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I start a generator with the load connected?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Starting under load strains the alternator, can stall the engine, and exposes connected equipment to the voltage and frequency fluctuation of a starting engine. Unplug everything and switch the panel breakers off first, then start, warm up for two to three minutes, and add loads afterwards."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What order should I plug things into a generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Largest starting draw first, then down to electronics last, adding one at a time and letting each settle. Motors like well pumps and fridge compressors briefly draw several times their running watts, so putting them on first means the generator has full headroom for that inrush rather than being pushed over the limit."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How far should a generator be from the house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Around twenty feet is the commonly cited minimum, with the exhaust directed away from the building and no doors, windows, vents or air intakes within that radius. Remember the openings that are easy to overlook: dryer vents, bathroom extractors, window air conditioners and basement hopper windows."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I leave a generator running while I sleep?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only with working carbon monoxide alarms on every level of the house and the machine properly placed well away from any opening. Many people instead run in blocks, since a full freezer holds about 48 hours unopened and a fridge about four, which means an overnight shutdown costs nothing and buys quiet."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do you turn off a generator properly?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Disconnect loads one at a time, switch the panel breakers off, let the engine run for a minute or two with no load so it cools evenly, then stop it with the engine switch. If you are storing it for more than a few weeks, close the fuel valve first and let it run until it stops, which empties the carburettor."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should I run my generator when there's no outage?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Start it and run it under a modest load for fifteen to twenty minutes every month or two. A generator exercised regularly is far more likely to start when you actually need it, because it keeps fuel moving through the carburettor and the battery charged on electric-start models. Almost nobody does this, and it is the cheapest reliability you can buy."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
