        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The order is: place it outdoors well away from the house with the exhaust pointing away, check oil and fuel, start it with nothing connected, warm it for two to three minutes, then add loads one at a time with the biggest starting draw first. To stop, reverse it — loads off, run a minute at no load, then switch off.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            One of those steps protects people and the rest protect equipment. Carbon monoxide from a generator run in a garage, under a deck or too close to a window is what actually kills people during outages, and no amount of correct load sequencing compensates for getting the placement wrong. Twenty feet, outdoors, exhaust away, CO alarms working inside.
          </p>
